==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|scharte|g=f}}, also {{m|gmh|schart|g=f|g2=m}}, from the adjective {{der|de|goh|*scart}} (“wounded, cut up”, attested in {{m|goh|liduscart}}), from {{der|de|gem-pro|*skardaz}}. Related with {{m+|de|scheren|t=to shear}}.

Cognate with {{cog|dum|schaert|g=m}}, {{cog|gml|schārt|g=n}}, {{cog|ang|sceard|g=n}}, {{cog|non|skarð|g=n}}. All possibly from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*skardą}}, although the deviating genders in Dutch and High German may suggest independent derivations from the adjective. See {{cog|en|sherd}}, {{m|en|shard}} for more.

